-          "quote": "Let $f,g$ define a regular index-one DAE and let $U\\times V$ be a selected local branch chart from [[thm-index-one-dae-implicit-function-reduction]], so $g(t,x,z)=0$ is equivalent to $z=\\psi(t,x)$ there. Input $t_0,x_0$, a seed $z_{\\mathrm{seed}}\\in V$, an algebraic residual tolerance $\\varepsilon_a\\ge0$, and a finite Newton-correction budget $N\\in\\mathbb N_0$. If $(t_0,x_0)\\notin U$, $g(t_0,x_0,\\cdot)$ is undefined at the seed, or dimensions do not match, return invalid-input.\n\nCall [[alg-finite-budget-newton-for-vector-residuals]] on the square residual $G(z)=g(t_0,x_0,z)$, with initial value $z_{\\mathrm{seed}}$, tolerance $\\varepsilon_a$, and budget $N$, restricting evaluations to the chart's open $z$-domain. If it reports root, reevaluate $G(z_0)$ once: if that residual is exactly zero, return $(x_0,z_0,\\texttt{exact-consistent})$; otherwise return $(x_0,z_0,\\texttt{approximate-consistent})$ and its norm. Map singular-jacobian, invalid-trial, and iteration-budget-exhausted to the corresponding initialization statuses and return the last valid iterate.\n\nThe procedure makes no arbitrary-start convergence claim. approximate-consistent records a residual bound; it is not relabeled as exact consistency. Under the index-one branch hypotheses an exact consistent value exists locally, by [[thm-index-one-dae-implicit-function-reduction]], but the finite Newton budget may fail to find it.\n\nThe solve has $m$ algebraic unknowns. With budget $N$, the Newton supplier performs at most $N$ corrections, each using one $m\\times m$ Jacobian solve, and at most $N+1$ residual evaluations. A root status adds one residual reevaluation for recording, so the procedure uses at most $N+2$ residual evaluations. Residual/Jacobian assembly and linear-solve costs depend on the chosen backend.",
